1
00:00:00,505 --> 00:00:05,750
Meet Bizgent: an AI finance agent for small businesses, built on NEAR.

2
00:00:06,364 --> 00:00:12,274
Small businesses want AI to pay routine bills. But an AI with a wallet can drain it.

3
00:00:12,377 --> 00:00:17,032
With Bizgent, a smart contract sets the limits, not the AI.

4
00:00:17,645 --> 00:00:21,120
This is Jayflint Studio, on NEAR testnet.

5
00:00:21,225 --> 00:00:28,090
Its USDC balance is read live from the Bizgent vault contract, and every payment shown here was verified on chain.

6
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:37,515
The owner set a 2 USDC per-payment limit, a 5 USDC daily limit, and approved recipients, all stored in the contract.

7
00:00:38,134 --> 00:00:44,514
The agent account holds one function-call key. It can call exactly one method: agent_pay.

8
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:51,015
Now I ask the assistant, running on NEAR AI Cloud, to pay the cloud storage invoice.

9
00:00:53,119 --> 00:01:00,259
It finds the expense and proposes the payment. The policy allows it, so the agent pays 1 USDC.

10
00:01:00,368 --> 00:01:02,368
Confirmed on chain.

11
00:01:03,774 --> 00:01:09,964
On chain: the agent called agent_pay on the vault, and the vault sent USDC to the supplier.

12
00:01:16,029 --> 00:01:23,074
A 3 USDC licence is over the per-payment limit, so the AI can't pay it. It becomes an approval.

13
00:01:27,671 --> 00:01:34,061
The owner is emailed, but an email link never moves money. The owner reviews the exact payment...

14
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:40,366
...signs owner_pay with their own NEAR key, and Bizgent verifies it on chain before marking it paid.

15
00:01:40,977 --> 00:01:51,977
The limits are real: on testnet, the contract rejected an over-limit payment, an unapproved recipient, a replayed payment ID, a wrong caller, and a revoked agent.

16
00:01:52,588 --> 00:01:59,563
Bizgent: contract-enforced limits for AI money. Try it at bizgent.vercel.app.

17
00:01:59,674 --> 00:02:02,814
What limits would you give your AI finance agent?
